<Base title="Roadmap"
      eyebrow="What is left, in the order it is worth doing"
      dek="The question this archive was built to answer has been answered. This page says what replaced it, ranks the ways at the new one, and marks plainly which routes are finished, which are live, and which need a person rather than a search.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The question that started it, and its answer</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Who were Pablo Armando Lerena&rsquo;s parents?</strong> For most of this archive&rsquo;s
      life that was the whole of it. The line below him was closed and documented; above him there was
      nothing at all, and the Argentine record refused three times over.</p>
    <p>It was answered by a document he filled in himself. His <strong>1908 Argentine army enrolment
      card</strong> names them: <strong>Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> and <strong>María Lerena</strong>.
      Corroborated by his eldest son being christened <strong>Ricardo Juan Carlos</strong>, and
      independently by a granddaughter&rsquo;s DNA, which carries a Río de la Plata genetic group that can
      only have come through him.</p>
    <p class="cap">It is also the archive&rsquo;s worst near-miss. The card was
      <strong>written off for a month</strong> on four objections, three of which turned out to be
      artefacts of a machine index. Opening the image took two minutes.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>The card &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="The question that replaced it" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Who were Juan Carlos and María Lerena, and where did they come from?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Two names on a card, and nothing behind them. <strong>No baptism, no marriage, no census entry, no
      death</strong> for either of them has been found anywhere. And the reason that mattered so much on
      13 September is that the last place they could plausibly have hidden was checked, and they are not
      in it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Read in full</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The Rosario baptism index, 1879&ndash;1901.</strong> End to end. No Lerena in
        any spelling, and no Pablo Armando under any surname in 1881, 1882 or 1883.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Read in full</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The Rosario baptism index, 1731&ndash;1879.</strong> Eighty openings. The
        surname occurs <strong>once</strong>, and she is Uruguayan.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Read in full</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The Rosario marriage index, 1867&ndash;1885.</strong> No Lerena. They did not
        marry in that parish.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px"><strong>That is the whole surviving parish record of the city,
    a hundred and seventy years of it, and the family is not in any of it.</strong> A household that lives
    in a parish leaves baptisms. This one leaves nothing &mdash; which is no longer a gap in the searching.
    It is a fact about the family.</div>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="The answer that now leads" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>They were not a Rosario family &mdash; and the one Lerena in that parish was Uruguayan</h2>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Strong lead, no link proved &mdash; 13 September 2026</span></p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p>The single Lerena baptism in the whole index, <strong>folio 229, February 1866</strong>, was opened
      the same day it was found. The act says of both parents:
      <em><strong>&ldquo;naturales del E. Oriental&rdquo;</strong></em> &mdash; natives of Uruguay. The
      father is a <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong>, the mother a <strong>Juanicó</strong>, and the
      godfather &mdash; another Don Luis Lerena &mdash; stood <strong>by proxy</strong>, which is what one
      does from the other side of the river.</p>
    <p>And the family that produces is one this archive had been circling from the opposite direction for
      four days: the sons of <strong>Luis Lerena y Fernández</strong> and <strong>Justiniana Lenguas
      González</strong> of Montevideo, one of whom is <strong>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</strong>,
      co-founder of the <strong>Argentine Stud Book</strong> &mdash; already in the register, marked
      <em>high interest</em>, because <strong>Pablo Armando trained and owned racehorses</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>Nothing links Juan Carlos Lerena to that house.</strong> But it explains four separate
      refusals at once, and it says where to look.
      <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>Hypothesis 1c &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/horses/")}>The horses &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="Routes, ranked by what they would settle" />

<section class="blk">
  <h3 style="margin-top:8px">1 · Montevideo <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">the household is open; he is not in it</span></h3>
  <p>Worked hard on 13 September, and it produced more than it was asked for. <strong>The one documented
    Lerena household of that generation in the River Plate is now open from the marriage act to the last
    burial</strong>, every member on a primary record:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The marriage</strong> &mdash; San Francisco de Asís, Ciudad Vieja, act nº 258,
      <strong>19 December 1864</strong>. <em>Don Luis Lerena y Lenguas, hijo legítimo de Don Luis y de
      Doña Justiniana Lenguas</em>, with <em>Doña Julia Juanicó, hija legítima del Sr. Don Cándido y de
      Doña Juliana González</em>. The join the whole argument rested on, and it had rested on a tree.</li>
    <li><strong>Seven children</strong>, not five &mdash; María Julia (b. Rosario 1865, m. Jaime
      Herrera 1886); <strong>Laura Adela</strong> (b. 1868); Luis (d. 1897, aged 25); Enrique
      (d. 1914); Ema Sixta (b. <strong>21 Nov 1876</strong>, m. José Pedro Massera 1913);
      <strong>José Julio</strong> (b. 15 Mar 1879); and <strong>Cándido Jaime</strong> (b. 13 Sep
      1884, m. María Concepción Magariños Pittaluga 1922). The last two were found on the evening of
      13 September. The five had been built outward from Julia&rsquo;s death certificate &mdash; and a
      death certificate names the children who mattered to whoever registered the death, not the
      children a woman bore.</li>
    <li><strong>Cándido Jaime&rsquo;s baptism, act nº 1720, names four grandparents in one line</strong>
      &mdash; paternal <em>Don Luis</em> and <em>Doña Justiniana Lenguas</em>, maternal <em>Don Cándido</em>
      and <em>Doña Juliana González</em> &mdash; and his godparents are his own eldest sister María and
      <strong>Don Jaime Herrera, whom she married in this same parish four months later</strong>. They
      stood at the font betrothed.</li>
    <li><strong>Laura Adela&rsquo;s baptism names every grandparent</strong> independently of the marriage
      act and agreeing with it exactly &mdash; and gives his full name, <strong>Luis Eugenio</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Julia&rsquo;s baptism</strong> &mdash; born <strong>23 November 1842</strong>, christened
      4 October 1843, San Francisco de Asís. Her mother in full: <em>Juliana Joaquina González</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s death</strong> &mdash; <strong>24 December 1901</strong>, seven in the
      morning, calle Suárez nº 25, el Paso del Molino, act nº 216. <em>&ldquo;De nacionalidad oriental,
      nacido en Montevideo, de cincuenta y seis de edad, de estado casado, y de profesión
      <strong>hacendado</strong>.&rdquo;</em> A landowner &mdash; the first statement of this family&rsquo;s
      standing anywhere in the archive. His mother <strong>Justiniana Lenguas was alive at 79</strong> and
      in the house.</li>
    <li><strong>Julia&rsquo;s death</strong> &mdash; 11 July 1910, calle Constituyente 992, Montevideo,
      <strong>a widow</strong>, which his 1901 act now explains exactly.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>And the household above it opened too.</strong>
    Searching on <em>Justiniana Lenguas</em> as mother &mdash; the rarest stable name in a family the
    clerks write as Lerena, Lorena, Serena and Languas &mdash; returned four siblings of Luis Eugenio,
    and <strong>Gilberto Lerena among them</strong>. Two records of Gilberto&rsquo;s own children give his
    parents as Luis and Justiniana Lenguas. So <strong>the co-founder of the Argentine Stud Book is a
    brother of the man whose daughter was baptised at Rosario in 1866</strong>. The horses and the Rosario
    thread are one family. Index-level, and stated as a strong lead rather than a proved descent until
    the images are opened.</div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><strong>And Juan Carlos Lerena is still not among them.</strong> Seven
    children and five siblings now named in those two generations, and he is in none of them. He is not
    in the indexed Uruguayan record either &mdash; no Juan Carlos Lerena born there 1830&ndash;1870 in the
    church records, the civil registration or the baptism index; no Lerena groom marrying a María
    1865&ndash;1885; and exactly <strong>two</strong> full-text hits for the phrase in the whole corpus,
    both twentieth-century.</p>
  <p><strong>What is still open here.</strong> <em>Cándido Juanicó&rsquo;s first marriage</em> to Juliana
    Joaquina González as a document &mdash; though the two-wives inference is now confirmed from the
    children themselves, Julia born 1842 to Juliana against Sara, Zara and Celia born 1866&ndash;69 to
    <em>Sixta Lenguas</em>. The <em>images</em> behind Gilberto&rsquo;s parentage and behind José
    Julio&rsquo;s birth. And the reading that full text cannot do for you: the Montevideo marriage
    registers of the 1870s, turned page by page, for a Juan Carlos marrying a María.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">2 · The consulate that issued the passport <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">letter sent, 9 Sep 2026 — no reply yet</span></h3>
  <p>To be issued an Argentine passport in 1940 he had to prove nationality, so the Cape Town consulate
    held a <em>matrícula consular</em> on him, and those files went home to the <strong>Archivo Histórico
    de Cancillería</strong>. Sent to <span class="mono">archivo@cancilleria.gob.ar</span>, an address taken
    from the Cancillería&rsquo;s own contact page rather than guessed. It asks whether the matrícula
    survives, whether the 1940 passport file survives and <strong>whether it names his parents</strong>,
    and &mdash; flagged as minor &mdash; whether he ever held a consular appointment, which puts the
    family&rsquo;s Consul General tradition to the only body that could settle it.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">3 · The rest of his own army file <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">letter sent, with the card attached</span></h3>
  <p>The 1908 enrolment card is an index card. Behind it sits a <em>legajo</em> &mdash; the service file
    &mdash; which would carry his birthplace, his parents&rsquo; details in full, and quite possibly his
    parents&rsquo; own origins. Written to the <strong>Archivo General del Ejército</strong> with the card
    itself attached, so they have the reference number rather than a description of it.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">4 · He came with the horses <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">untried, and still the biggest idea here</span></h3>
  <p>We have been hunting a <strong>passenger</strong>. He may never have been one. The South African War
    shipped some <strong>360,000 horses</strong> into the country, <strong>Argentina among the main
    sources</strong>. He was <strong>seventeen when it began and twenty when it ended</strong>, a horseman
    by trade and a horse-dealer at the Cape by 1911. Horses travel with <strong>conductors, grooms and
    muleteers</strong>, engaged at the port of loading, and those men are written down in
    <strong>military remount paperwork, not civilian passenger lists</strong> &mdash; which would explain
    why he is invisible in every passenger index searched.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">Where: <strong>The National Archives, Kew &mdash; WO 108</strong> and related
    Remount Department series; the Cape remount depots, chiefly <strong>Stellenbosch</strong>; and the
    British purchasing commissions at Buenos Aires. <strong>None of it has been touched.</strong></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">5 · The 1935 mission census <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">needs a person in Brisbane</span></h3>
  <p>Both younger sons appear in an <strong>LDS South African Mission census of 1935</strong>, each indexed
    as <strong>&ldquo;Son&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; which means a <strong>head of household</strong> stands on
    that page, and it should be Pablo Armando, giving his own birthplace <strong>five years before the
    passport and independent of it</strong>. The image is not online; it needs a reader at an affiliate
    library. Why two Catholic boys of eleven and fifteen are in a Mormon mission census in the year their
    mother died is a question in itself.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">6 · The Ley 4144 expulsion files <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">needs David, and a passport</span></h3>
  <p>The family tradition is that he left Argentina as a political refugee. The Ley 4144 of 1902 deported
    <em>foreigners</em>, which is the flaw in the story and also the test of it. The
    <strong>Archivo General de la Nación</strong> portal needs an account; the Spanish text is written and
    ready to paste. <a href={u("/errands/")}>The errands &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">7 · Rosario, again <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">closed — do not reopen without a new reason</span></h3>
  <p>Both volumes of the baptism index read end to end. 1881, 1882 and 1883 read across
    <strong>all twenty-six letter sections</strong> for a <em>Pablo Armando</em> under any surname
    whatsoever &mdash; roughly two hundred image-reads, more than twenty near misses logged and excluded,
    including the only two Armandos in three years. The marriage index read at full resolution. The 1895
    national census: not one Lerena in Santa Fe.</p>
  <p><strong>And on 13 September the index itself was put on trial.</strong> Every Rosario null here
    rests on a typescript compiled in <strong>1945</strong>, and whether that typescript is complete had
    never been tested. So the <strong>register</strong> was read act by act, from the margins at full
    resolution: <strong>acts 113 to 862, 1 February to 22 July 1882, seven hundred and fifty
    baptisms</strong>. Everywhere the index says there is no Lerena, the register says the same.
    <strong>The index and the register agree</strong> &mdash; so he is not there, and the index is not
    the reason. <a href={u("/searched/")}>The sweep &rarr;</a></p>
  <p><strong>This route is exhausted, not merely unsuccessful.</strong></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="five-reels">Five reels, named &mdash; the 1791 marriage is filmed after all</h2>
  <p class="dek">For weeks this archive recorded Sancho&rsquo;s marriage as unreachable. That verdict came
    from a full-text search, and full text covers a fraction of what is filmed.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>The index entry has now been read.</strong> Canelones, <em>&iacute;ndice de matrimonios
      1776&ndash;1947</em>, <strong>DGS 8166578, image 2531</strong>, under <em>Letra L</em> and the
      sub-heading <em>Libro I</em>: <strong>&ldquo;Llerena Sancho con Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez,
      1791&rdquo;</strong>, with the year column beside it running 1777, 1778, 1780, 1781, 1783, 1785,
      1786, 1787, 1788, 1789, 1790, <strong>1791</strong>, 1792 in order. Until that morning the date came
      from Apolant&rsquo;s ficha 355 and the <em>venue</em> came from nowhere &mdash; this archive supplied
      the word &ldquo;Canelones&rdquo; itself.</p>
    <p><strong>The act is on the same reel &mdash; and on 15 September 2026 it was read.</strong>
      <em>Matrimonios, Libros 1&ndash;3, 1776&ndash;1854</em> (items 11&ndash;13) opens at
      <strong>image 2590</strong> behind a target board at 2586. The marriage of <strong>Sancho Llerena and
      Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez, 8 January 1791</strong>, is at <strong>image 2687</strong>, left-hand
      page, second act: <em>&ldquo;En ocho d&iacute;as del Mes de Enero de <strong>1791</strong>
      a&ntilde;os&hellip; Sancho Llerena, nat[ura]l de <strong>C&oacute;rdova la Llana</strong>, hijo
      leg&iacute;timo de <strong>F&eacute;lix Llerena</strong> y de <strong>Rosal&iacute;a
      Aguilar</strong>&hellip;&rdquo;</em> The year is in arabic numerals. It was found by reading dated
      anchors rather than by arithmetic &mdash; <strong>2670 is 12 February 1789 and 2700 is 16 June
      1792</strong>, about nine images to the year. See <a href="/TheLerena/hypotheses/">the
      hypotheses page</a> and <code>data/1791-marriage-act.tsv</code>.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:16px">The five books that cover January 1791</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>DGS</th><th>Where it came from</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Canelones &mdash; &Iacute;ndice de matrimonios 1776&ndash;1947</strong> <span class="cap">the entry is here</span></td><td>8166578</td><td rowspan="3">Catalogue <code>koha:1294117</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Canelones &mdash; Matrimonios, Libros 1&ndash;3, 1776&ndash;1854 <span class="cap">the act &mdash; FOUND, image 2687</span></td><td>8166578</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Canelones &mdash; Informaciones matrimoniales, Carpetas 1&ndash;6, 1776&ndash;1806 <span class="cap">walked &mdash; does not cover January 1791</span></td><td>8166573</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Montevideo &mdash; Libros Castrenses: &ldquo;del Regimiento de Infanter&iacute;a de Buenos Ayres&rdquo;, 1781&ndash;1814</strong> <span class="cap">opened &mdash; item 5, from image 623; 1791 is at image 660</span></td><td>7713373</td><td rowspan="2">Catalogue <code>koha:961086</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Montevideo &mdash; &Iacute;ndices de bautismos, matrimonios y defunciones, 1726&ndash;1800 <span class="cap">attribution NOT confirmed &mdash; images 300 and 1500 are 1887 and 1905 burials</span></td><td>7713386</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Why the military books are no longer a hopeful
      idea.</strong> The Canelones baptism of Sancho&rsquo;s son Pedro Antonio, <strong>1 August 1793</strong>,
      was performed by <em>&ldquo;el Capell&aacute;n del <strong>Regimiento de Infanter&iacute;a de Buenos
      Ayres</strong> Don Bartolom&eacute; Mu&ntilde;oz&rdquo;</em> and calls Sancho <em>&ldquo;el
      <strong>soldado drag&oacute;n</strong>&rdquo;</em>. That is the same regiment named in the catalogue
      entry for DGS 7713373. The unit whose chaplain was serving this family at Canelones two years after
      the wedding kept its own marriage register across 1791, and it is filmed.</div>
    <h3 id="informaciones-walked" style="margin-top:26px">The informaciones matrimoniales, walked &mdash; and the answer is no</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A <em>pre-marriage investigation</em> was the best remaining hope for Sancho&rsquo;s parents: it
        routinely gives a Spanish groom&rsquo;s parents, his birthplace and how long he had been in the
        country, on oath. On <strong>15 September 2026</strong> the reel was read by image number rather
        than through the viewer, and the result is a clean negative.</p>
      <p><strong>The series does not cover January 1791.</strong> Its filming targets divide it into
        volumes: <strong>Volumen 6 runs 1776 to November 1790</strong> and ends at image 1459;
        <strong>Volumen 7 opens at image 1460</strong> and its first dated expediente is
        <strong>March 1791</strong>. Sancho married on <strong>8 January 1791</strong>. The whole of 1791
        in this series is a single expediente belonging to somebody else &mdash; <em>Juan Manuel
        S&aacute;nchez</em>, an Andalusian marrying Mar&iacute;a Medina, images 1462&ndash;1464 &mdash; and
        by image 1464 the archivist&rsquo;s pencil already reads <em>&ldquo;Marzo 1792&rdquo;</em>.</p>
      <p>So his own <em>informaci&oacute;n</em> is not there. That is a <strong>seam in the filing, not a
        failed search</strong>, which is worth stating precisely because the two look identical from a
        distance. One caveat is real: the filming card at image 1461 reads
        <strong>&ldquo;HOJAS SUELTAS&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; loose leaves. The order is whatever order
        somebody filed them in, so a stray 1791 sheet elsewhere in Volumen 7 is not impossible. It is
        simply not where it should be.</p>
      <div class="note"><strong>What the series did give.</strong> At <strong>image 2167</strong>,
        <strong>9 January 1798</strong>, Sancho Llerena is sworn as the <strong>second witness</strong> for
        Florentino G&oacute;mez &mdash; not the groom, as this archive had left open. He had
        <em>&ldquo;andado en la L&iacute;nea de Vicar&iacute;a algunos a&ntilde;os&rdquo;</em>. And the
        deposition closes: <em>&ldquo;y no la firm&oacute; <strong>porque dixo no saver
        escrivir</strong>&rdquo;</em>. <strong>Sancho Llerena could not write.</strong> That is a primary
        fact about the man himself, and it sits pointedly beside the 1811 baptism that calls him
        <em>&ldquo;Don Sancho Llerena&rdquo;</em> &mdash; an illiterate <em>Don</em> is a man whose
        standing came from something other than schooling.</div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Which is why the castrense books now go first.</strong>
        A serving soldier&rsquo;s marriage needed his commander&rsquo;s licence and frequently ran through
        the military chaplaincy rather than the parish <em>vicar&iacute;a</em>. That would explain, with no
        appeal to loss or accident, why Sancho appears in this series as a <em>witness</em> and never as a
        <em>petitioner</em>. The full walk, image by image, is in
        <code>data/informaciones-1791-walk.tsv</code>.</p>
    </div>
    <h3 id="castrense-opened" style="margin-top:26px">The castrense books, opened &mdash; one attribution held, one did not</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>DGS 7713373 is not what this archive called it</strong>, and is nonetheless the right
        film. Its own boards read <em>&ldquo;Uruguay, Archivo de la Curia&rdquo;</em> and
        <em>&ldquo;Montevideo, Archivos Parroquiales, P. Inmac. Concepci&oacute;n (Catedral)&rdquo;</em>,
        project URUG 1000A, roll 84. The military material is <strong>item 5</strong>, titled
        <em>&ldquo;Cat&aacute;logo y Libros Militares&rdquo;</em>, beginning at <strong>image 623</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>The regiment is named in the priest&rsquo;s own hand</strong>, at image 655:
        <em>&ldquo;yo Don Juan Miguel de Berroeta, <strong>Capell&aacute;n del Regimiento Infanter&iacute;a
        de Buenos Ayres</strong>, en esta Iglesia Matriz de Montevideo&hellip;&rdquo;</em> Neighbouring
        entries name the <strong>Dragones de Buenos Ayres</strong> &mdash; and Sancho is
        <em>&ldquo;el soldado drag&oacute;n&rdquo;</em> in 1793, so his attested unit is inside this
        book&rsquo;s scope rather than adjacent to it.</p>
      <p><strong>And the book covers the month he married.</strong> Image 656 is headed <em>A&ntilde;o
        1790</em>; <strong>image 660 is headed <em>A&ntilde;o de 1791</em></strong> and carries an entry of
        30 January 1791; image 661 carries 27&ndash;28 March 1791. The book runs from about image 652 to
        about 950 and ends in 1814. <strong>Nothing named Llerena was found in the five images read &mdash;
        and five images is not a search of the book.</strong> Reading it properly is about three hundred
        images, and it is the next job.</p>
      <p><strong>The 1789&ndash;1791 window has since been read continuously</strong> &mdash; images
        652 to 662, eleven openings without a gap, covering December 1787 to July 1791 and so the month
        Sancho married. <strong>No Llerena, Lerena or Lorena in any of them.</strong> The families in the
        book in these years are Menchero, Arias, Illescas, Lamas, S&aacute;nchez, Mart&iacute;nez, Coronel,
        Villar and Ju&aacute;rez &mdash; Manchegan, Galician, Andalusian and porte&ntilde;o soldiers, and a
        great many baptisms of enslaved children belonging to the regiment&rsquo;s officers.</p>
      <p><strong>That is a negative over eleven images, not over the book</strong>, and it is a scan rather
        than a transcription. Images 663&ndash;950 are unread. This hand was misread at fit-width three
        times in one sitting &mdash; the chaplain as &ldquo;Mendoza&rdquo; when he is <strong>Berroeta</strong>,
        a slave-owner as &ldquo;Llerena&rdquo; when he is <strong>Francisco Ju&aacute;rez</strong>, and a
        mother as &ldquo;Rosal&iacute;a Illera&rdquo; when she is <strong>Petrona Illescas</strong>. Every
        candidate beginning with <em>L</em> was magnified before being dismissed, but a name buried
        mid-sentence in an unzoomed line could have been missed. <em>Not found</em> and <em>not there</em>
        are different statements.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-bottom:14px"><strong>And there is a one-search test that should be
        run before anyone reads another three hundred images.</strong> FamilySearch full text was working
        again on 15 September 2026 &mdash; 68 results for <em>&ldquo;Sancho Llerena&rdquo;</em>, none from
        any military collection. That proves nothing until a <strong>control</strong> is run: search a
        phrase known to be in this book, such as <em>&ldquo;Capell&aacute;n del Regimiento Infanter&iacute;a
        de Buenos Ayres&rdquo;</em> or the chaplain&rsquo;s name <strong>Berroeta</strong>. If it returns
        hits, the book is indexed and Sancho&rsquo;s absence is a real finding won in one search. If it
        returns nothing, the book is not in full text and the remaining images must be read by hand.
        <strong>The control could not be run: Error 15 returned before it could be sent, and no zero from a
        blocked page has been recorded as a null.</strong></div>
      <div class="note"><strong>DGS 7713386 did not hold up.</strong> This archive called it
        <em>&ldquo;&Iacute;ndices de bautismos, matrimonios y defunciones, 1726&ndash;1800&rdquo;</em> and
        called opening it <em>the cheapest test in the archive</em>. Image <strong>300 is a burial register
        of the Catedral Bas&iacute;lica de Montevideo for 1887</strong>; image <strong>1500 is a numbered
        burial register for 1905</strong>. Neither is an index and neither is eighteenth century. Two
        samples out of 2,244 images do not prove the index is absent from the film &mdash; but the
        attribution is unconfirmed, and it should no longer be called cheap or certain. Both attributions
        came from a catalogue reading rather than from the films.</div>
    </div>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>How the catalogue was reached at all.</strong> Its search
      box returned &ldquo;Something Went Wrong&rdquo; for every query for a whole day. A <em>direct
      catalogue ID</em> still worked &mdash; and better, <strong>any image&rsquo;s <em>Information</em>
      tab prints the catalogue link and that reel&rsquo;s item-by-item contents</strong>. That is how both
      record numbers above were recovered without the search box. One warning for whoever goes next: image
      2631 of the Canelones reel opens under the page title <em>&ldquo;Colombia, Catholic Church
      Records&rdquo;</em> and cites the Archdiocese of Cartagena. The image is Canelones; its own
      Information panel says so.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What would close it fastest</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Route 1, as soon as FamilySearch lets this session back in.</strong> It costs nothing but
      time, it is the first place the evidence now points, and it is the only route that could name Juan
      Carlos Lerena&rsquo;s own parents rather than merely confirm his existence.</p>
    <p><strong>Routes 2 and 3 are already out</strong> and can only be waited on. If both come back empty,
      <strong>route 4</strong> is where the real thinking has to go &mdash; because if he came to Africa
      with a cargo of horses rather than a ticket, then no passenger list will ever have him, and we have
      been looking in the wrong archive from the beginning.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:26px">
    <p class="lab">14 September 2026 &mdash; and one route away from Pablo Armando is now precisely
      specified</p>
    <p>Everything above is about the Argentine end. The Uruguayan end has a single document that would
      answer more questions than any other, and it has never been opened: <strong>Sancho&rsquo;s own
      marriage, 8 January 1791</strong>. It would settle whether he was <em>Sancho</em> or
      <em>Francisco</em>, give his parents and his Spanish birthplace in his own generation&rsquo;s words,
      and test both <em>Aguilar</em> and C&oacute;rdoba at once.</p>
    <p><strong>The date has a source and the place never did.</strong> The 8 January 1791 comes from
      <strong>Apolant, <em>Operativo Patagonia</em> (Montevideo, 1970), ficha 355</strong>. The word
      <em>Canelones</em> comes from nowhere &mdash; this archive supplied it. So the question is not
      &ldquo;find the Canelones act&rdquo; but <strong>which surviving books could hold a marriage on that
      day</strong>. The Instituto de Estudios Geneal&oacute;gicos del Uruguay publishes a holdings list,
      and it gives four answers:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Canelones, Catedral de Nuestra Se&ntilde;ora de Guadalupe</strong> &mdash; marriages from
        <strong>1775</strong>, and <em>informaciones matrimoniales</em> from 1776. Still the leading
        candidate: the 1812 padr&oacute;n makes all six children <em>&ldquo;naturales del
        Canel&oacute;n&rdquo;</em>, the eldest born about 1791. And Sancho has <em>already</em> been found
        inside that investigation series, giving sworn evidence at somebody else&rsquo;s marriage.</li>
      <li><strong>Montevideo, Iglesia Matriz</strong> &mdash; ordinary books from 1727, and
        <strong>three <em>Libros Castrenses</em> covering 1781&ndash;1814</strong>.
        <strong>Sancho was a <em>soldado</em></strong>, recorded so since the first day, from the Uruguayan
        census. A soldier marrying in the Banda Oriental in 1791 has an obvious second home for his act,
        in books kept by the chaplaincy rather than the parish &mdash; and <strong>nobody has ever looked
        in them.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>The Curia, Arzobispado de Montevideo</strong> &mdash; <em>expedientes de informaci&oacute;n
        matrimonial</em> from 1727. <strong>This is the document worth most</strong>: a pre-marriage
        investigation routinely gives a Spanish groom&rsquo;s parents, birthplace and time in the country,
        which is more than the marriage act itself usually carries.</li>
      <li><strong>San Jos&eacute; de Mayo &mdash; raised and eliminated the same day.</strong> The
        <em>Operativo Patagonia</em> convoy&rsquo;s 41 Asturian founder families &mdash; <strong>seven of
        them from Morc&iacute;n</strong>, the concejo where the bride was born in 1778 &mdash; founded San
        Jos&eacute; on 1 June 1783, so her family plausibly landed there. But its
        <strong>marriage books begin in 1799</strong>, eight years too late. Its baptisms run from 1783 and
        are worth asking about for the family&rsquo;s arrival.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="cap">A caution on the date itself: the bride is given as born 1778, which would make her
      twelve, while the 1812 padr&oacute;n implies about fifteen. Whoever searches should be asked to look
      a year either side. A published source can still be wrong.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">Nothing here unseats the passport. It is his own document,
    sworn at a consulate, naming a city, a province and a day &mdash; and the enrolment card, filled in
    thirty-two years earlier, agrees with it. What the parish record refuses to do is corroborate either
    one. An archive that says so plainly is worth more than one that quietly stops looking.</div>
</section>
